Comment (collector.go:745):
**Low — bidirectional substring match can over-attach IAM credential-removal events.** `strings.Contains(principal, ident) || strings.Contains(ident, principal)` is broad on the second clause. Concretely: a CloudTrail event whose `userName` is a short identifier like `ad`, `svc`, or `a` will substring-match \*every\* resource-policy principal whose ARN happens to contain those letters (`arn:aws:iam::123:user/**ad**min`, `arn:aws:iam::123:role/**svc**-rotator`, etc.), so the deleted-credential event attaches to unrelated secrets. The forward direction `Contains(principal, ident)` already handles the normal "username embedded in principal ARN" case; the reverse direction was presumably added to catch shorthand account-id principals (`"AWS": "123456789012"` matched by `userArn` containing that account). Suggest narrowing the reverse to that case explicitly (e.g. exact account-id segment match), or anchoring matches to ARN segment boundaries (split on `:`/`/` and compare segments) so 2–3 char usernames cannot fan out across unrelated principals. The design note in `git notes` describes this match as one-directional ("affected IAM user/role identifier substring-matches a secret resource-policy principal"); the bidirectional implementation is broader than the documented intent.
